## How to Verify a Marquise Halo Before You Buy

Three checks every ring must pass before you commit.

**Check 1 — Confirm halo shape matches marquise.** The halo inside opening must be marquise-cut, not round or oval. The 360° ring video shows this clearly. If the halo is round or oval, pass or request a custom shape-matched halo.

**Check 2 — Confirm centre-to-halo gap under 0.5 mm.** Look at the stone-and-halo photo closely. The first row of halo accents should sit tight against the centre girdle. A visible dark line between stone and halo kills the 1.5x visual trick.

**Check 3 — Confirm centre prong spec is V-tip six-prong.** The halo hides the centre prongs visually but does nothing structurally. V-tip six-prong at the centre is mandatory on any halo setting. The retailer will spec this at order for $0 extra on most listings. See [IGI](https://www.igi.org/) and [GIA 4Cs](https://4cs.gia.edu/) for centre stone certification standards.

I run these three checks before I quote any client on a halo ring. The halo is beautiful when shape-matched, tight-set, and paired with V-tip centre prongs. It is a $2,000 wasted investment when any one of those three fails.

